[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Secure Data Storage in Android: EncryptedSharedPreferences and Android KeyStore"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When building secure Android applications, storing sensitive data like authentication tokens, API keys, or user credentials securely is paramount. Standard SharedPreferences stores data in plain XML files, making them easily readable on rooted devices. To solve this, Android provides the Security library, which combines EncryptedSharedPreferences and the Android KeyStore system to encrypt data at rest."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"How Android KeyStore Works"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Android KeyStore system lets you store cryptographic keys in a container to make it more difficult to extract from the device. Keys are generated hardware-backed (when available), meaning they never leave the secure hardware boundary. EncryptedSharedPreferences uses these keys to transparently encrypt keys and values before writing them to disk."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Step-by-Step Implementation in Java"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"First, add the Jetpack Security dependency to your app-level build.gradle file. Ensure your minSdk is set to 23 or higher, as the cryptographic features heavily rely on the Android KeyStore."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"implementation "androidx.security:security-crypto:1.1.0-alpha06""},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Initializing EncryptedSharedPreferences"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"To initialize EncryptedSharedPreferences, you must first create or retrieve a MasterKey from the Android KeyStore. This key will be used to encrypt both the preference keys and their corresponding values."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"try {
    MasterKey masterKey = new MasterKey.Builder(context)
            .setKeyScheme(MasterKey.KeyScheme.AES256_GCM)
            .build();

    SharedPreferences sharedPreferences = EncryptedSharedPreferences.create(
            context,
            "secure_prefs_filename",
            masterKey,
            EncryptedSharedPreferences.PrefKeyEncryptionScheme.AES256_SIV,
            EncryptedSharedPreferences.PrefValueEncryptionScheme.AES256_GCM
    );

} catch (GeneralSecurityException | IOException e) {
    e.printStackTrace();
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Writing and Reading Data"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once initialized, EncryptedSharedPreferences behaves exactly like standard SharedPreferences. You can use standard editor methods to write data securely."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"// Writing data
SharedPreferences.Editor editor = sharedPreferences.edit();
editor.putString("auth_token", "secret_token_12345");
editor.apply();

// Reading data
String token = sharedPreferences.getString("auth_token", null);"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices and Security Considerations"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Exception Handling: Always wrap initialization in try-catch blocks because cryptographic operations can throw GeneralSecurityException or IOException if the KeyStore gets invalidated."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Device Lock Screen: For higher security, consider tying the MasterKey creation to user authentication (biometrics or lock screen) using setUserAuthenticationRequired(true)."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Performance: Encryption and decryption introduce minor performance overhead. Avoid reading or writing from EncryptedSharedPreferences inside tight loops or on the main UI thread unnecessarily."}]
